  PRIMARY  the CALIBRATED cell from sensefit: sigma 56.0 Hz at tau 60 ms,
           at the shipped 905 ms round trip
           open  107.9   best   51.6 at gain 0.00100   removed  52.2%   CLEARS THE 25% BAR
           measured trial accuracy here 0.753  (selfcode 0.623, sensefit's own decoder 0.764)
           same sigma at the other correlation times:  0 ms 55.8%   60 ms 52.2%  

           BLIND (no sensor)      37.2 Hz   <- feed-forward reach alone
           the measured sensor    51.6 Hz    +14.5 Hz against blind
           a PERFECT sensor       47.5 Hz    +10.4 Hz against blind
           -> of the 107.9 Hz the open loop starts with, REACH removes
              70.7 Hz and FEEDBACK then ADDS BACK 14.5 Hz
           bracket on sigma: 0 Hz -> 55.9%,  92 Hz -> 48.9%  BOTH clear the bar

  GUARD  correlation axis live AT THE OPERATING POINT?  white removes 55.8%
         and the measured tau 52.2%, 3.7 points apart -> INERT HERE. Read the top of the
         sigma ladder for where it bites: correlation costs nothing at the
         measured noise and a great deal at 2-4x it, so the operating point
         sits below the level where the error's TIME STRUCTURE matters.

  FEEDBACK LOSES TO A BLIND CONTROLLER AT THE SHIPPED DELAY, and so
  does a PERFECT sensor. A constant bias per target has no loop, so it
  has no stability limit and is DELAY-IMMUNE; a 905 ms loop is neither.
  Feedback wins only at delays this creature does not have -- at 0 ms a
  perfect sensor reaches 6.0 Hz against blind's 37.2.

  THIS CORRECTS THIS EXPERIMENT'S OWN EARLIER HEADLINE. Scoring arms
  against gain 0 measured REACH, which a sensorless controller collects
  in full, and the 25%-of-open-loop bar was cleared by exactly that.
  What survives is the sensor characterisation and the gain-delay
  budget; what does not is 'the ear is good enough to steer by'.
